# Generated rule outputs (dustin-thomason)

The source of truth is `.cursor/rules/*.mdc`. Two downstream outputs are generated from it and **must never be hand-edited**:

- **`AGENTS.md`** — committed, machine-neutral mirror for Codex (full rule bodies).
- **`.claude/rules/*.md`** — per-machine, git-ignored rules for Claude Code (full body, pointer stanza, or path-scoped, depending on the rule).

`scripts/sync-rules.ps1` regenerates both; direct edits to either are overwritten on the next run.

When you **create, edit, rename, or delete** any file under `.cursor/rules/` or `.cursor/skills/` in this repo:

1. Run **`.\scripts\sync-rules.ps1`** from the repo root before you finish.
2. Stage the regenerated **`AGENTS.md`** with the same work — do not leave the mirror stale. `.claude/rules/` is git-ignored and rebuilt per machine, so it is not staged.

A brand-new rule must also be classified in `sync-rules.ps1` (`$Manifest`: `full` | `pointer` | `scoped`), or the script fails loudly rather than silently dropping it from an output.

The `scripts/git-hooks/pre-commit` hook runs this automatically at commit time when hooks are enabled (`git config core.hooksPath scripts/git-hooks`), so regeneration is enforced no matter which agent or human edited the rules. Running the sync by hand is the fallback when hooks are not set up.

```
Good: edit .cursor/rules/<rule>.mdc, then run .\scripts\sync-rules.ps1, then stage AGENTS.md.
Bad:  hand-editing AGENTS.md or .claude/rules/*.md (the next sync silently overwrites it).
```
